1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:08,090
Our raw data is twelve parquet files, one per month of 2025.

2
00:00:08,370 --> 00:00:12,460
They are too big for git, so they stay in a data folder that git ignores.

3
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:20,369
We load them once into a DuckDB table, raw green trips, and a source, in sources dot yml, tells dbt about it.

4
00:00:21,319 --> 00:00:26,036
The second file is small: a lookup of the 265 taxi zones.

5
00:00:26,316 --> 00:00:29,004
Small CSV files like this one are seeds.

6
00:00:29,284 --> 00:00:33,086
They live in the repository, and dbt loads them as tables.

7
00:00:34,036 --> 00:00:38,218
The files come from the New York City Taxi and Limousine Commission website.

8
00:00:38,498 --> 00:00:44,677
Under 2025, download Green Taxi Trip Records for each month, January to December.

9
00:00:44,957 --> 00:00:46,445
The links are in the description.

10
00:00:47,395 --> 00:00:52,089
Further down the same page, download the Taxi Zone Lookup Table, as CSV.

11
00:00:53,039 --> 00:00:56,115
In Studio, open the nyc taxi project.

12
00:00:56,395 --> 00:00:59,625
Right-click the project, choose New Folder, and call it data.

13
00:01:02,574 --> 00:01:04,931
Inside data, make a folder called raw.

14
00:01:08,379 --> 00:01:14,223
Now move the twelve parquet files into data, raw, and the zone lookup CSV into seeds.

15
00:01:14,503 --> 00:01:16,609
Refresh the explorer, and there they are.

16
00:01:17,559 --> 00:01:19,633
Now load the files into DuckDB.

17
00:01:19,913 --> 00:01:24,102
Open the SQL Editor, and pick the nyc taxi dev connection.

18
00:01:26,266 --> 00:01:27,960
Three statements, one per line.

19
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:29,728
Create a schema called raw.

20
00:01:30,008 --> 00:01:37,123
Create the table raw green trips, from read parquet over all twelve files: use the full path of your data raw folder.

21
00:01:37,403 --> 00:01:38,495
Then count the rows.

22
00:01:39,445 --> 00:01:42,811
Run them one by one, with the green arrow next to each line.

23
00:01:43,091 --> 00:01:48,422
Union by name lines up the columns, even if a month's file has them in a different order.

24
00:01:55,940 --> 00:02:03,585
Five hundred ninety-one thousand, three hundred seventy-five trips in 2025, now in one DuckDB table.

25
00:02:04,535 --> 00:02:07,516
Back in the project, tell dbt about the table.

26
00:02:07,796 --> 00:02:11,323
Right-click models, New File, and call it sources dot yml.

27
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:17,569
A source called raw, the schema we just made, with a short description.

28
00:02:18,519 --> 00:02:22,402
It has one table, green trips: one row per green taxi trip.

29
00:02:22,682 --> 00:02:23,281
Save.

30
00:02:23,561 --> 00:02:27,232
In a model, source raw, green trips, now points at that table.

31
00:02:28,182 --> 00:02:29,210
Now the Git tab.

32
00:02:29,490 --> 00:02:33,351
Only two files changed: sources dot yml and the CSV.

33
00:02:33,631 --> 00:02:38,145
The parquet files are not here, because the gitignore leaves out the data folder.

34
00:02:39,095 --> 00:02:44,231
Stage all, type a message, declare the raw trips source and add the zone lookup, and commit.

35
00:02:47,785 --> 00:02:49,201
Then click Sync Changes.

36
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:51,434
Studio pushes the commit to GitHub.

37
00:02:52,885 --> 00:02:58,807
On GitHub, the repository now has the models and seeds folders, and the latest commit message.

38
00:02:59,757 --> 00:03:06,352
To recap: the trip files sit in data raw, outside git, and live in DuckDB as raw green trips.

39
00:03:06,632 --> 00:03:08,017
The zone lookup is a seed.

40
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:10,686
And sources dot yml declares the source.

41
00:03:10,966 --> 00:03:13,330
Next, we build our first model on top of it.
